{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-a370bcf8-2743-46d4-acf1-4d615b511bf5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-81d5c9efbfc74096","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9020-6z8me016/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9020-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9020\n\n## Strategy Overview\n\nAMD GPU (ROCm) enablement for serving and training. Two strategic customers\nstandardize on AMD Instinct accelerators; the platform ships only CUDA runtime\nbuilds and has no first-hand ROCm parity data. The strategy mandates a\nhardware validation campaign (HLR-1) whose go/no-go output scopes productizing\na vLLM ROCm serving runtime (HLR-2), PyTorch training support on ROCm through\nKueue (HLR-3), and customer-facing documentation (HLR-4).\n\n## Triage\n\nNot below threshold: the strategy spans four components, two teams, and three\nwork-product types (validation campaign, code productization, documentation),\nwith HLRs at three priority levels. Not documentation-only: HLR-1..3 require\ncode and validation work. Full decomposition follows.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | AMD Instinct validation campaign: vLLM ROCm functional and performance parity | Investigation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 | Productize supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 | PyTorch ROCm training support through the Training Operator | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 | Kueue quota handling for AMD GPUs (amd.com/gpu) | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 | Document AMD GPU (ROCm) enablement: hardware, drivers, validated models | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P1 |\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 Validation campaign] --> E002[RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 vLLM ROCm runtime productization]\n    E001 --> E005[RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 Documentation]\n    E002 --> E005\n    E003[RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 PyTorch ROCm training] --> E005\n    E004[RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 Kueue amd.com/gpu quota] --> E005\n```\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The campaign's go/no-go output defines which models the productized runtime must support; the productization epic's scope is rewritten by the outcome. True gate (`rewrite`): if parity fails for some or all models, the productization scope shrinks or the epic is removed. Recorded via `gated_by` and `gate_failure_impact` on E002. |\n| E001 → E005 | Rule 11 + content dependency | Documentation must state the validated model list, which only exists once the campaign reports. |\n| E002 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs blocked by all Implementations) | Docs describe the productized runtime. |\n| E003 → E005 | Rule 11 | Docs cover supported training configuration. |\n| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 | Docs cover quota setup for AMD GPUs. |\n| E001 ∥ E003, E001 ∥ E004 (non-edges) | Rules 14/17 | The campaign validates vLLM serving; the training-side epics are independent work in different repos (training-operator, kueue) with no shared artifact. No gate: training support is delivered regardless of serving parity outcomes. |\n| E003 ∥ E004 (non-edge) | Rules 1/14 | Different components (Training Operator vs. Distributed Workloads), different repos, no shared artifact — Kueue quota configuration does not build against Training Operator code. Parallel; merge-conflict/coordination risk only. |\n| E002 ∥ E003, E002 ∥ E004 (non-edges) | Rule 14 | Different repos and components; no shared build artifacts. |\n\nNo conditional branch files: the campaign's outcome space (which subset of the\nsupported model set clears parity) adjusts one epic's scope rather than\nselecting between different downstream epics, so the gate is expressed as\n`gated_by` + `rewrite` on E002, not as `-BRANCH-` alternatives.\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | Validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware; produces parity data and the go/no-go model scope. Hardware access (procurement/partner-lab) is an externally-controlled availability precondition recorded as a risk on E001, not an acceptance criterion. |\n| HLR-2 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 | Productization of the vLLM ROCm serving runtime for campaign-cleared models. Gated by E001 (`rewrite`). KServe is listed as an affected component but implies no KServe code change — serving the new runtime under KServe InferenceServices is folded in as a validation acceptance criterion on this epic (Step 2 passive-component rule; same owning team). |\n| HLR-3 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E003, RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 | Single HLR spanning two components owned by the same team; split by component boundary (Rule 1). E003 covers PyTorchJob single/multi-GPU execution on ROCm; E004 covers Kueue ClusterQueue/LocalQueue quota handling for `amd.com/gpu`. Both are P1 (no P0 inheritance — neither blocks P0 work). |\n| HLR-4 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 | `docs-authoring` epic; priority derived via the docs exception from strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1. Blocked by all Implementation epics plus the campaign (source of the validated model list). Includes mandatory technical-review AC. |\n\nAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. No HLR required\npriority-split bundling judgments (HLR-3's split is architectural, by\ncomponent, not by priority).\n\n## Component Mappings\n\nAll epic `component` values are verbatim canonical names from\n`.context/rhai-components.txt`. Non-obvious mappings:\n\n- **KServe** (strategy affected component) → passive. No HLR implies KServe\n  code change; the ServingRun