le — but the division of validation responsibility (E003: job runnability under queueing; E004: amd.com/gpu quota accounting and enforcement) could be stated more explicitly to avoid duplicated test effort.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (0/2)\nEpic types are correct: E001 is a genuine Investigation (its go/no-go output rewrites E002's scope and would remove it entirely on total failure), and E002–E005 produce concrete artifacts. E002's gating metadata is exemplary: `gated_by: E001` is in its direct dependencies and `gate_failure_impact` has both `action: rewrite` and a populated fallback.\n\nHowever, **E005 lists the Investigation epic E001 in its `dependencies` while leaving `gated_by: null`** — an Investigation dependency without `gated_by`. This is a major issue, not a benign content dependency: the campaign's outcome determines the validated-model-list content E005 documents, and in the total-failure branch (E002 removed per its own gate_failure_impact) E005's scope is rewritten — the \"installing and using the ROCm serving runtime\" topic loses its subject and the epic instead documents the failed-parity outcome. By the framework's own test (an outcome that changes the downstream epic's scope on failure is a gate, and every Investigation dependency implies one), E005 must set `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-9020-E001` with a `gate_failure_impact` (e.g. action: rewrite; fallback: document the failed-parity outcome and explicitly unsupported model list rather than productized-runtime usage). The asymmetry with E002 — which gates on the same campaign for the same reason — is the visible symptom. Correction: add `gated_by` and `gate_failure_impact` to E005 (or, if the team judges docs content merely reflects whatever the outcome is, remove E001 from E005's dependencies and rely on the E002 edge — but that breaks the total-failure branch where E002 is removed while HLR-4 still requires documenting the result, so setting the gate is the correct fix).\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\nSignal sets are type-correct: E001 carries `investigation_signals` (5-signal rubric); E002–E005 carry `ai_signals` (9-signal rubric); no epic mixes them. Values check out against the rubrics and the strategy: E001's `cluster_hardware_dependence: -2` (the gating parity question requires live AMD Instinct hardware), `source_accessibility: 0` (the answer exists in no readable artifact — it must be produced by running the campaign), `human_judgment_required: -1` (procurement is peripheral, the gating question is technical) are all rubric-consistent. E002's `open_questions: -1` / `external_dependency: -1` correctly carry the upstream ROCm cadence open question. Architecture-claims calibration verified against `.context`: serving-runtimes.md corroborates \"only CUDA (NVIDIA) builds are currently productized — ROCm ... exist upstream but are not [shipped]\" (E002, E005); training-operator.md corroborates PyTorchJob/Kueue-admission/GPU-node premises (E003); distributed-workloads.md corroborates the ClusterQueue/LocalQueue quota model and waitForPodsReady gang admission (E004). All five ai-signals rationale files are present with justifications matching the frontmatter values. (Arithmetic/thresholds not checked — computed by the pipeline.)\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\nEvery epic has testable ACs derived from HLR text. Rule-mandated ACs are present where applicable: E005 has the technical-review sign-off AC (Rule 26); E002 has the build-pipeline-green AC (no konflux chain exists, but the equivalent midstream-pipeline AC is present anyway); no epic replaces existing behavior, so rollback/feature-flag ACs are not mandated. The canonical soft-AC trap is handled correctly: hardware access is recorded as a risk on E001 (and E003/E004), not as an AC — \"dedicated AMD Instinct nodes are available\" appears nowhere as an acceptance criterion, and ACs that reference AMD GPU nodes condition what the epic itself verifies. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID (E001–E005 appear only in frontmatter and the summary).\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\nAll strategy scope is covered: HLR-1–HLR-4 map to epics; both open questions (hardware access, upstream ROCm cadence) are carried forward as epic risks and Health Warnings rather than silently dropped; out-of-scope items (Gaudi, GPU sharing) are respected; all four affected components are accounted for, with the KServe-passive judgment recorded and grounded in kserve.md's runtime-generic surfaces. The strategy has no Prerequisites & Process Gates section, but HLR-4 explicitly requires documentation and E005 exists with the correct docs-authoring shape. Cross-epic consistency holds: the HLR-3 split accounts for both the Training Operator side and the Kueue quota side, and E005's topics collectively cover everything E001–E004 deliver. The campaign's outcome space is bounded by E002's gate_failure_impact (partial clear → shrink; total failure → remove), with no unstated outcomes — the one gap (what E005 does in the total-failure branch) is the Type Correctness finding above.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":38919,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bdf1e7979e9c1f7e","agent_id":"ac6b1845a0d7d2121","session_id":"3c5f0377-4b7e-4251-9827-8f66cc239d73","uuid":"77bd0493-a3e0-43ba-93a6-427871f6aa72","timestamp":"2026-10-26T13:38:20.088Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9020 decomposition"}
