{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a271deaade64fc37","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9020\n10\t\n11\t## Strategy Overview\n12\t\n13\tAMD GPU (ROCm) enablement for serving and training. Two strategic customers\n14\tstandardize on AMD Instinct accelerators; the platform ships only CUDA runtime\n15\tbuilds and has no first-hand ROCm parity data. The strategy mandates a\n16\thardware validation campaign (HLR-1) whose go/no-go output scopes productizing\n17\ta vLLM ROCm serving runtime (HLR-2), PyTorch training support on ROCm through\n18\tKueue (HLR-3), and customer-facing documentation (HLR-4).\n19\t\n20\t## Triage\n21\t\n22\tNot below threshold: the strategy spans four components, two teams, and three\n23\twork-product types (validation campaign, code productization, documentation),\n24\twith HLRs at three priority levels. Not documentation-only: HLR-1..3 require\n25\tcode and validation work. Full decomposition follows.\n26\t\n27\t## Epic List\n28\t\n29\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n30\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n31\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | AMD Instinct validation campaign: vLLM ROCm functional and performance parity | Investigation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n32\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 | Productize supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n33\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 | PyTorch ROCm training support through the Training Operator | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n34\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 | Kueue quota handling for AMD GPUs (amd.com/gpu) | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n35\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 | Document AMD GPU (ROCm) enablement: hardware, drivers, validated models | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P1 |\n36\t\n37\t## Dependency DAG\n38\t\n39\t```mermaid\n40\tgraph TD\n41\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 Validation campaign] --> E002[RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 vLLM ROCm runtime productization]\n42\t    E001 --> E005[RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 Documentation]\n43\t    E002 --> E005\n44\t    E003[RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 PyTorch ROCm training] --> E005\n45\t    E004[RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 Kueue amd.com/gpu quota] --> E005\n46\t```\n47\t\n48\t## DAG Justification\n49\t\n50\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n51\t|------|------|-----------|\n52\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The campaign's go/no-go output defines which models the productized runtime must support; the productization epic's scope is rewritten by the outcome. True gate (`rewrite`): if parity fails for some or all models, the productization scope shrinks or the epic is removed. Recorded via `gated_by` and `gate_failure_impact` on E002. |\n53\t| E001 → E005 | Rule 11 + content dependency | Documentation must state the validated model list, which only exists once the campaign reports. |\n54\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs blocked by all Implementations) | Docs describe the productized runtime. |\n55\t| E003 → E005 | Rule 11 | Docs cover supported training configuration. |\n56\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 | Docs cover quota setup for AMD GPUs. |\n57\t| E001 ∥ E003, E001 ∥ E004 (non-edges) | Rules 14/17 | The campaign validates vLLM serving; the training-side epics are independent work in different repos (training-operator, kueue) with no shared artifact. No gate: training support is delivered regardless of serving parity outcomes. |\n58\t| E003 ∥ E004 (non-edge) | Rules 1/14 | Different components (Training Operator vs. Distributed Workloads), different repos, no shared artifact — Kueue quota configuration does not build against Training Operator code. Parallel; merge-conflict/coordination risk only. |\n59\t| E002 ∥ E003, E002 ∥ E004 (non-edges) | Rule 14 | Different repos and components; no shared build artifacts. |\n60\t\n61\tNo conditional branch files: the campaign's outcome space (which subset of the\n62\tsupported model set clears parity) adjusts one epic's scope rather than\n63\tselecting between different downstream epics, so the gate is expressed as\n64\t`gated_by` + `rewrite` on E002, not as `-BRANCH-` alternatives.\n65\t\n66\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n67\t\n68\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n69\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n70\t| HLR-1 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | Validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware; produces parity data and the go/no-go model scope. Hardware access (procurement/partner-lab) is an externally-controlled availability precondition recorded as a risk on E001, not an acceptance criterion. |\n71\t| HLR-2 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 | Productization of the vLLM ROCm serving runtime for campaign-cleared models. Gated by E001 (`rewrite`). KServe is listed as an affected component but implies no KServe code change — serving the new runtime under KServe InferenceServices is folded in as a validation acceptance criterion on this epic (Step 2 passive-component rule; same owning team). |\n72\t| HLR-3 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E003, RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 | Single HLR spanning two components owned by the same team; split by component boundary (Rule 1). E003 covers PyTorchJob single/multi-GPU execution on ROCm; E004 covers Kueue ClusterQueue/LocalQueue quota handling for `amd.com/gpu`. Both are P1 (no P0 inheritance — neither blocks P0 work). |\n73\t| HLR-4 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 | `docs-authoring` epic; priority derived via the docs exception from strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1. Blocked by all Implementation epics plus the campaign (source of the validated model list). Includes mandatory technical-review AC. |\n74\t\n75\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. No HLR required\n76\tpriority-split bundling judgments (HLR-3's split is architectural, by\n77\tcomponent, not by priority).\n78\t\n79\t## Component Mappings\n80\t\n81\tAll epic `component` values are verbatim canonical names from\n82\t`.context/rhai-components.txt`. Non-obvious mappings:\n83\t\n84\t- **KServe** (strategy affected component) → passive. No HLR implies KServe\n85\t  code change; the ServingRuntime/InferenceService surfaces are generic over\n86\t  runtimes per `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`.\n87\t  Validation that the ROCm runtime serves under KServe is an acceptance\n88\t  criterion on E002 (same owning team, RHOAI Serving).\n89\t- **Documentation** (E005) is a canonical component with no architecture\n90\t  context file; team assigned as RHOAI Serving (owner of the primary\n91\t  documented surface) — the strategy provides no Impacted Teams table to\n92\t  confirm a dedicated docs team.\n93\t- E001's component is Serving Runtimes (the campaign's subject is vLLM\n94\t  serving); its scope touches KServe only as the deployment surface under\n95\t  test.\n96\t\n97\t## Health Warnings\n98\t\n99\t- **Missing strategy sections**: no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies\n100\t  table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, no Non-Functional\n101\t  Requirements. Team assignments were derived from architecture-context\n102\t  owning-team fields; per-epic ACs were derived from HLR text.\n103\t- **Externally-controlled hardware access (open question 1)**: dedicated AMD\n104\t  Instinct nodes do not exist in the CI fleet; procurement or partner-lab\n105\t  access is unresolved and outside the strategy's control. Recorded as a risk\n106\t  on E001 (and a validation risk on E003/E004), not as an AC or DAG edge —\n107\t  the campaign epic exists and its deliverable is the report either way.\n108\t- **Upstream ROCm image cadence (open question 2)**: AMD/upstream release\n109\t  timing is outside our control and may not align with release trains.\n110\t  Recorded as a risk on E002 with a pinned-and-validated-image acceptance\n111\t  criterion; it does not change epic existence or structure, so no\n112\t  Investigation epic was created for it.\n113\t- **Cluster-side AMD GPU enablement is a precondition, not decomposed\n114\t  scope**: none of the four affected components owns the AMD GPU device\n115\t  plugin / node provisioning. E001, E003, and E004 treat AMD GPU nodes with a\n116\t  working `amd.com/gpu` resource as an environment precondition (risk/notes);\n117\t  creating a provisioning epic would invent scope outside the stated\n118\t  components.\n119\t- **Docs team assumption**: E005 assigned to RHOAI Serving for lack of an\n120\t  Impacted Teams table; substitute the actual docs owner at planning time.\n121\t\n122\t## Tiered Delivery\n123\t\n124\tThe campaign outcome (and upstream ROCm cadence) has uncertain timing, so\n125\tdelivery is tiered:\n126\t\n127\t- **Tier 1 (independent, start immediately)**: E001 (risk-reduction\n128\t  validation campaign), E003 and E004 (training-side enablement, independent\n129\t  of serving parity outcomes).\n130\t- **Tier 2 (gated)**: E002 — serving runtime productization, gated by the\n131\t  E001 go/no-go scope (`rewrite`).\n132\t- **Last**: E005 — documentation of what was built and validated.\n133\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-92a829e20fcd2861","agent_id":"a0fbfd4fec95aca89","session_id":"3c5f0377-4b7e-4251-9827-8f66cc239d73","uuid":"705507c1-a520-4beb-87f3-8e8887d82321","timestamp":"2026-10-26T13:39:39.474Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a271deaade64fc37","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9020 decomposition"}
