{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-959717d83df501aa","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9020\n11\t\n12\t## Strategy Overview\n13\t\n14\tAMD GPU (ROCm) enablement for serving and training. Two strategic customers\n15\tstandardize on AMD Instinct accelerators; the platform ships only CUDA runtime\n16\tbuilds and has no first-hand ROCm parity data. The strategy mandates a\n17\thardware validation campaign (HLR-1) whose go/no-go output scopes productizing\n18\ta vLLM ROCm serving runtime (HLR-2), PyTorch training support on ROCm through\n19\tKueue (HLR-3), and customer-facing documentation (HLR-4).\n20\t\n21\t## Triage\n22\t\n23\tNot below threshold: the strategy spans four components, two teams, and three\n24\twork-product types (validation campaign, code productization, documentation),\n25\twith HLRs at three priority levels. Not documentation-only: HLR-1..3 require\n26\tcode and validation work. Full decomposition follows.\n27\t\n28\t## Epic List\n29\t\n30\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n31\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n32\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | AMD Instinct validation campaign: vLLM ROCm functional and performance parity | Investigation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n33\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 | Productize supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n34\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 | PyTorch ROCm training support through the Training Operator | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n35\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 | Kueue quota handling for AMD GPUs (amd.com/gpu) | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n36\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 | Document AMD GPU (ROCm) enablement: hardware, drivers, validated models | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P1 |\n37\t\n38\t## Dependency DAG\n39\t\n40\t```mermaid\n41\tgraph TD\n42\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 Validation campaign] --> E002[RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 vLLM ROCm runtime productization]\n43\t    E001 --> E005[RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 Documentation]\n44\t    E002 --> E005\n45\t    E003[RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 PyTorch ROCm training] --> E005\n46\t    E004[RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 Kueue amd.com/gpu quota] --> E005\n47\t```\n48\t\n49\t## DAG Justification\n50\t\n51\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n52\t|------|------|-----------|\n53\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The campaign's go/no-go output defines which models the productized runtime must support; the productization epic's scope is rewritten by the outcome. True gate (`rewrite`): if parity fails for some or all models, the productization scope shrinks or the epic is removed. Recorded via `gated_by` and `gate_failure_impact` on E002. |\n54\t| E001 → E005 | Rule 11 + content dependency | Documentation must state the validated model list, which only exists once the campaign reports. True gate (`rewrite`), recorded via `gated_by` and `gate_failure_impact` on E005: in the total-failure branch (no models clear parity, productization removed), the docs scope is rewritten to document the failed-parity outcome and an explicitly unsupported model list instead of productized-runtime usage. |\n55\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs blocked by all Implementations) | Docs describe the productized runtime. |\n56\t| E003 → E005 | Rule 11 | Docs cover supported training configuration. |\n57\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 | Docs cover quota setup for AMD GPUs. |\n58\t| E001 ∥ E003, E001 ∥ E004 (non-edges) | Rules 14/17 | The campaign validates vLLM serving; the training-side epics are independent work in different repos (training-operator, kueue) with no shared artifact. No gate: training support is delivered regardless of serving parity outcomes. |\n59\t| E003 ∥ E004 (non-edge) | Rules 1/14 | Different components (Training Operator vs. Distributed Workloads), different repos, no shared artifact — Kueue quota configuration does not build against Training Operator code. Parallel; merge-conflict/coordination risk only. Division of validation responsibility across the shared Kueue admission surface is explicit: E003 validates job runnability under queueing (admitted jobs run correctly); E004 validates `amd.com/gpu` quota accounting and enforcement — no duplicated test effort. |\n60\t| E002 ∥ E003, E002 ∥ E004 (non-edges) | Rule 14 | Different repos and components; no shared build artifacts. |\n61\t\n62\tNo conditional branch files: the campaign's outcome space (which subset of the\n63\tsupported model set clears parity) adjusts scope rather than selecting between\n64\tdifferent downstream epics, so the gates are expressed as `gated_by` +\n65\t`rewrite` on E002 (productization scope shrinks or is removed) and on E005\n66\t(docs rewrite to the failed-parity outcome in the total-failure branch), not\n67\tas `-BRANCH-` alternatives.\n68\t\n69\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n70\t\n71\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n72\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n73\t| HLR-1 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | Validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware; produces parity data and the go/no-go model scope. Hardware access (procurement/partner-lab) is an externally-controlled availability precondition recorded as a risk on E001, not an acceptance criterion. |\n74\t| HLR-2 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 | Productization of the vLLM ROCm serving runtime for campaign-cleared models. Gated by E001 (`rewrite`). KServe is listed as an affected component but implies no KServe code change — serving the new runtime under KServe InferenceServices is folded in as a validation acceptance criterion on this epic (Step 2 passive-component rule; same owning team). |\n75\t| HLR-3 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E003, RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 | Single HLR spanning two components owned by the same team; split by component boundary (Rule 1). E003 covers PyTorchJob single/multi-GPU execution on ROCm; E004 covers Kueue ClusterQueue/LocalQueue quota handling for `amd.com/gpu`. Both are P1 (no P0 inheritance — neither blocks P0 work). |\n76\t| HLR-4 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 | `docs-authoring` epic; priority derived via the docs exception from strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1. Blocked by all Implementation epics plus the campaign (source of the validated model list). Gated by the campaign (`rewrite`): if no models clear parity, the usage topic is replaced by the failed-parity outcome and an explicitly unsupported model list. Includes mandatory technical-review AC. |\n77\t\n78\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. No HLR required\n79\tpriority-split bundling judgments (HLR-3's split is architectural, by\n80\tcomponent, not by priority).\n81\t\n82\t## Component Mappings\n83\t\n84\tAll epic `component` values are verbatim canonical names from\n85\t`.context/rhai-components.txt`. Non-obvious mappings:\n86\t\n87\t- **KServe** (strategy affected component) → passive. No HLR implies KServe\n88\t  code change; the ServingRuntime/InferenceService surfaces are generic over\n89\t  runtimes per `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`.\n90\t  Validation that the ROCm runtime serves under KServe is an acceptance\n91\t  criterion on E002 (same owning team, RHOAI Serving).\n92\t- **Documentation** (E005) is a canonical component with no architecture\n93\t  context file; team assigned as RHOAI Serving (owner of the primary\n94\t  documented surface) — the strategy provides no Impacted Teams table to\n95\t  confirm a dedicated docs team.\n96\t- E001's component is Serving Runtimes (the campaign's subject is vLLM\n97\t  serving); its scope touches KServe only as the deployment surface under\n98\t  test.\n99\t\n100\t## Health Warnings\n101\t\n102\t- **Missing strategy sections**: no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies\n103\t  table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, no Non-Functional\n104\t  Requirements. Team assignments were derived from architecture-context\n105\t  owning-team fields; per-epic ACs were derived from HLR text.\n106\t- **Externally-controlled hardware access (open question 1)**: dedicated AMD\n107\t  Instinct nodes do not exist in the CI fleet; procurement or partner-lab\n108\t  access is unresolved and outside the strategy's control. Recorded as a risk\n109\t  on E001 (and a validation risk on E003/E004), not as an AC or DAG edge —\n110\t  the campaign epic exists and its deliverable is the report either way.\n111\t- **Upstream ROCm image cadence (open question 2)**: AMD/upstream release\n112\t  timing is outside our control and may not align with release trains.\n113\t  Recorded as a risk on E002 with a pinned-and-validated-image acceptance\n114\t  criterion; it does not change epic existence or structure, so no\n115\t  Investigation epic was created for it.\n116\t- **Cluster-side AMD GPU enablement is a precondition, not decomposed\n117\t  scope**: none of the four affected components owns the AMD GPU device\n118\t  plugin / node provisioning. E001, E003, and E004 treat AMD GPU nodes with a\n119\t  working `amd.com/gpu` resource as an environment precondition (risk/notes);\n120\t  creating a provisioning epic would invent scope outside the stated\n121\t  components.\n122\t- **Docs team assumption**: E005 assigned to RHOAI Serving for lack of an\n123\t  Impacted Teams table; substitute the actual docs owner at planning time.\n124\t\n125\t## Tiered Delivery\n126\t\n127\tThe campaign outcome (and upstream ROCm cadence) has uncertain timing, so\n128\tdelivery is tiered:\n129\t\n130\t- **Tier 1 (independent, start immediately)**: E001 (risk-reduction\n131\t  validation campaign), E003 and E004 (training-side enablement, independent\n132\t  of serving parity outcomes).\n133\t- **Tier 2 (gated)**: E002 — serving runtime productization, gated by the\n134\t  E001 go/no-go scope (`rewrite`).\n135\t- **Last**: E005 — documentation of what was built and validated.\n136\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ae49ff7a34a034a3","agent_id":"a15de2d1f4fd63bdd","session_id":"3c5f0377-4b7e-4251-9827-8f66cc239d73","uuid":"fe7f2069-002d-4ba8-be41-3aa69a27ab91","timestamp":"2026-10-26T13:40:54.841Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-959717d83df501aa","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review revised decomposition"}
